            {
                "type": "text",
                "content": "A verb changes its form to tell us WHEN something happens — in the past, now, or in the future. Getting the tense right keeps your writing clear. Quick words: tense means the part of a verb that shows when something happens, like now, before, or later.",
                "cognition": "knowing"
            },
            {
                "type": "key",
                "content": "Make the simple past of most verbs by adding -ed: today I walk, yesterday I walked.",
                "cognition": "knowing"
            },
            {
                "type": "example",
                "content": "Making the past tense.",
                "steps": [
                    "Take the verb 'play'.",
                    "Add -ed for the past: 'played'.",
                    "Yesterday I played football."
                ],
                "cognition": "knowing"
            },
            {
                "type": "fillblank",
                "prompt": "Yesterday I ___ in the yard. (play)",
                "answer": "played",
                "explain": "Add -ed to make the simple past of 'play': played.",
                "rule": "Make the simple past of most verbs by adding -ed.",
                "practiceItems": [
                    {
                        "prompt": "past tense of 'walk'",
                        "answer": "walked"
                    },
                    {
                        "prompt": "past tense of 'jump'",
